  "briefing": {
    "headline": "The simulation predicts two home days a week (Wednesday and Friday) would cut leavers over the next year from about 641 to 445, roughly 196 fewer departures (-31%), with average sentiment clearly positive (+1.0 on a -2 to +2 scale).",
    "most_at_risk": "No group reacts badly, but the gain is uneven: caregivers (-39%), managers (-36%) and long commuters (-35%) are most likely to stay, because the time saved goes straight to family and hours on the road, while short commuters (-26%) and juniors (-29%) see it as a nice bonus rather than a reason to stay.",
    "risks": [
      "Staff are counting on the promise that pay, reviews and promotion are unchanged. If home days later look like they hurt someone's promotion chances, or managers quietly discourage people from using them, the retention gain could disappear and trust could fall below today's level.",
      "Expectations may keep rising. Several people say they would prefer three days, so any later rollback or tightening would land worse than never offering the policy at all.",
      "The simulation only measures feelings and intent to leave. It says nothing about productivity, collaboration or how juniors learn on the job, so those effects are untested and should be watched separately."
    ],
    "design_changes": [
      "Make the two days a default entitlement for everyone in scope, not something each manager approves, so access does not depend on who your boss is.",
      "Put the 'no impact on reviews or promotion' commitment in writing. After the first review cycle, check that people who work from home are rated and promoted at the same rate as those who come in, and share the results.",
      "Plan the three office days on purpose, with team meetings, mentoring and junior coaching on Monday, Tuesday and Thursday. Track monthly leaving rates by group, especially caregivers, long commuters and juniors, and set a 6-month review point."
    ],
    "confidence": "medium, because every group and every sample voice points the same way, which makes the direction believable, but this is a synthetic model built on only 48 voices, the sample quotes are almost all from men and contain no dissent, so treat the 31% as a rough size rather than a forecast."
  },
